\section{Joint nested-Laplace engine — spatial-prior parameterisation}{

When fitted with \code{method = "nested_laplace"} and an areal spatial term in
the latent-presence formula (\code{bym2(graph = adj)}, or \code{car()} /
\code{car_proper()}), the engine identifies a single latent field \code{z} per region
and parameterises the two arms as \verb{eta_occ = X beta_occ + sigma * z}
and \verb{eta_pos = X beta_pos + alpha * sigma * z}. Both BYM2 sub-blocks
(\code{phi}, \code{theta}) are subject to hard sum-to-zero constraints (see
\code{.joint_inner_var()} for the math): the cover-arm intercept
\code{beta_pos[1]} is identified as the population mean of \code{eta_pos}
under \code{mean(z) = 0}. Any simulator generating data for this engine
must demean both \code{phi_f} and \code{theta_f} before scaling, otherwise the
estimator targets \code{beta_pos_0_truth + alpha * mean(w_s_sim)} and
coverage of the \emph{population} truth collapses with alpha. See
\code{\link[=simulate_cover_joint]{simulate_cover_joint()}} for a ready-made demeaned simulator.
}

\section{Spatially varying trend (a weighted areal term)}{

A spatially varying trend is model structure, so it is declared in the
formula as a SECOND, weighted areal term on the same graph as the intercept
field, not through \code{control}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ time.sc +
  icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell_idx") +
  icar(graph = adj, weight = time.sc, group_var = "cell_idx")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The unweighted term is the shared intercept field; the weighted term
\code{icar(..., weight = col)} is the trend field, whose contribution to each
arm's predictor is \code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}, coupled onto the cover arm with
its own scale (\code{alpha_trend}, reported in \code{fit$alpha_trend} /
\code{fit$sigma_trend}) integrated over the outer grid. The umbrella spelling
\code{spatial(graph = adj, model = "icar", weight = col)} resolves identically.
The trend coupling grid defaults to \code{control$alpha.grid}; override it with
\code{control$alpha.grid.trend}. Requires \code{method = "nested_laplace"}. A coupled
trend cannot currently combine with \code{temporal()} / \code{re()} blocks in the same
fit.
}

\section{Varying-coefficient spatial bar (the compact single-term form)}{

The intercept field plus its weighted trend field can also be written as one
\code{spatial()} term carrying an lme4-style coefficient formula and a \verb{to =}
argument naming the arms that share the field:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{~ time.sc + habitat +
  spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj,
          to = c("presence", "positive"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The bar left-hand side spells the coefficient fields: the intercept column
(\code{1}) is the unweighted shared field; each covariate column (\code{time.sc}) is a
weight-scaled coefficient field (\code{weight_i * z[cell_i]}). The bar right-hand
side (\code{cell_idx}) is the graph node index (the areal \code{group_var}); \code{||}
requests independent fields. This desugars to exactly the two-term weighted-
areal form above, so the two spellings give the same fit.

The cover hurdle's two arms are \code{presence} (the \code{y > 0} Bernoulli arm) and
\code{positive} (the \code{y | y > 0} arm); \code{summary()} and the coefficient output
print these same labels. \verb{to =} validates against this arm set and may be
omitted to mean both arms.

\strong{Copy versus free: one call versus separate calls.} Whether the two
arms share one latent field or each carry their own is set by how many
\code{spatial()} calls you write, not by an option.

\emph{Copy / shared} -- one \code{spatial()} call naming both arms in \verb{to =} is a
single latent field, presence-anchored, copied onto the positive arm with an
estimated coupling per coefficient field (\code{alpha} for the intercept field,
\code{alpha_trend} for the trend field), marginalized on the outer grid:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# one latent field, shared by both arms
spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj, to = c("presence", "positive"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{presence: eta_presence = ... + u_cell + time.sc * s_cell
positive: eta_positive = ... + alpha * u_cell + alpha_trend * time.sc * s_cell
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The same \code{u_cell} (intercept field) and \code{s_cell} (time-slope field) appear in
both arms; the positive arm sees them through the estimated multipliers.

\emph{Free / separate} -- one single-arm \code{spatial()} call per arm declares a
separate latent field on each arm, each with its own precision and no
cross-arm coupling (gcol33/tulpaObs#65):

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# two independent latent fields, one per arm
spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj, to = "presence") +
spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj, to = "positive")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{presence: eta_presence = ... + u_presence_cell + time.sc * s_presence_cell
positive: eta_positive = ... + u_positive_cell + time.sc * s_positive_cell
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A lone single-arm call (\code{to = "presence"}) puts a field on that arm only. The
free fit reports \code{sigma_armspecific}. Arm-specific fields are their own
spatial structure: they do not combine with a shared field, a weighted trend,
a correlated \code{|} bar, or \code{temporal()} / \code{re()} in the same formula, and at most
one targets each arm.

The \code{||} and \code{|} axis is separate from copy / free: \code{||} makes the intercept
and slope fields independent, while a single \code{|} makes them correlated (a free
cross-covariance, MCAR). A correlated \code{|} bar is copy-only and requires both
arms (gcol33/tulpaObs#64).

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{one spatial() call, to = both arms    one shared / copied latent (presence anchor, coupling estimated)
separate single-arm spatial() calls   separate / free latents, no coupling
||                                     independent intercept and slope coefficient fields
|                                      correlated (MCAR) coefficient fields, copy-only
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

With the response on the formula left-hand side (gcol33/tulpaObs#66), the
shared-field cover hurdle reads:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{tobs(cover.flat ~ time.sc + habitat +
       spatial(~ 1 + time.sc || cell_idx, graph = adj,
               to = c("presence", "positive")),
     data = dat, family = cover(positive = "beta"),
     method = "nested_laplace")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{A formula bar is a random effect, not a spatial field}{

A bare lme4 bar in the formula -- \code{(1 | cell)}, \code{(1 + x | cell)},
\code{(x || cell)} -- is a grouped random effect, not a spatial field, even when the
grouping factor names the areal graph nodes. The engine's inline-MCAR call
\code{tulpa::spatial(graph, ~ 1 + x | cell)} reads \verb{| cell} as a separable spatial
field, but the same spelling inside a \code{cover()} formula is parsed as an IID
random effect on \code{cell}. For a spatial field on the cells write either the
compact \code{spatial()} bar of the section above,
\code{spatial(~ 1 + x || cell, graph = adj)}, or the two-term weighted-areal form,
\verb{icar(graph = adj, group_var = "cell") +}
\code{icar(graph = adj, weight = x, group_var = "cell")}. When a formula carries a
bar whose grouping factor is also an areal term's \code{group_var}, \code{cover()} emits
a one-time message noting the bar is fitted as a random effect; suppress it
with \code{\link[base:suppressMessages]{base::suppressMessages()}}.
}

\section{Checkpoint / resume}{

A spatial cover-hurdle fit integrates over a large outer hyperparameter grid
and can run for hours. \code{control$checkpoint = list(path = "fit.ckpt", resume = TRUE)} makes the joint engine append each completed grid cell to \code{path}; a
\code{resume = TRUE} run loads the finished cells and solves only the rest,
reproducing the from-scratch fit, so a killed or rebooted fit resumes instead
of restarting. \code{resume = FALSE} starts a fresh file. Forwarded to
\code{\link[tulpa:tulpa_nested_laplace_joint]{tulpa::tulpa_nested_laplace_joint()}}.
}

